LOCAL HEADER-OCR RECOVERY -- run log
Tool: C:\PatoLex-scratch\_header_ocr.py  (+ _scan_headers.py, _validate_*.py, _apply_1866.py)
Goal: token-efficient LOCAL recovery of "missing" chapters whose page-TOP running-head
("CHAPTER N" / "Chap. N" / roman) was skipped/garbled by full-page OCR. No agent vision
tokens; no DB writes; additive only; merged/certified files untouched.

Engine: Tesseract 5.4.0 (C:\Users\patolex\AppData\Local\Tesseract-OCR\tesseract.exe)
Python: C:\PatoLex-scratch\ocr-engines\surya-venv\Scripts\python.exe (has pytesseract, PIL, PyMuPDF, surya)

================================================================================
TOOL DESIGN
================================================================================
[2026-06-21] DESIGN | _header_ocr.py | OK
  * header_ocr_page(img): crops top strips (fracs 0.10/0.14/0.18/0.22) of the page
    image, upscales 2x, runs Tesseract --oem 1 at psm 6/7/11, parses the first
    legible chapter number. Optional Surya fallback (lazy-loaded).
  * parse_chapter_from_text(): arabic ("CHAPTER 482","CHAP. 482","[Ch. 482]") and
    roman ("CHAP. XXVI","CHAPTER CCCCLXXXII"). roman_to_int handles archaic ADDITIVE
    forms (CCCC=400, XXXX=40, VIIII=9) AND standard subtractive (XL, CD, CM).
  * OCR-tolerance (critical for early-era): the chapter word "CHAP."/"Chap." is
    routinely corrupted by Tesseract to "Cuap."/"CHap."/"Cnap."/"Cuar." -- lead-in
    regex accepts C/O + 2-3 letters + (p|r). Roman tokens normalize O->C, U->V
    (the 'CCC' triple often OCRs as 'CCO'). A leading word-boundary + required
    separator suppress false hits from prose/dates ("March 12" no longer matches).
  * Parser self-test: 12/12 cases pass (incl. all additive-roman forms).

================================================================================
VALIDATION (go/no-go)  -- threshold: >=85% exact match
================================================================================
[2026-06-21] VALIDATE-ARABIC | year 1935 (production-1935-vol1-chapters, image_verified) | OK
  Ground truth: parsed_acts_merged.json (791 chapters, chapter_int -> source_page).
  Sampled 30 known chapters; ran header-OCR on each chapter's source-page image.
  RESULT: EXACT match 28/30 = 93.3%.  Off-by-1 = 2/30 (explained: chapter starts
  mid-page, so the very-top running head shows the PREVIOUS chapter). NOFIND 0,
  WRONG 0.
  VERDICT: GO (93.3% >= 85%).

[2026-06-21] VALIDATE-ROMAN | year 1865-66 (early-era roman) from PDF | OK (engine), WARN (source_page)
  Rendered known-chapter pages on demand from 1865-66_Statutes.pdf (PyMuPDF).
  FINDING 1 (engine works): the roman header path reads real printed headers
  correctly -- e.g. PDF pg101 "CHap. XXIV"=24, pg102 "Cuap. XXVI"=26, pg316
  "Cuar. CCOXXIX"=329 (after O->C normalization). The OCR-tolerant lead-in + roman
  normalization were REQUIRED; the first-cut regex (literal "CH") missed ~all of
  these and produced date/page-number false positives.
  FINDING 2 (data caveat -- DURABLE): for the early-era 1865-66 volume the merged
  parsed_acts_merged.json `source_page` is in the right coordinate system for
  PRESENT chapters (pg101->ch25, pg106->ch33/34 confirmed) BUT some recorded
  source_pages point at mid-act CONTINUATION pages with no header at all (e.g.
  ch13 "source_page 113" is continuation text, no "Chap." header). So a naive
  point-lookup on source_page is unreliable for the missing-chapter set; the
  manifest's page_RANGES (bracketed by reliable PRESENT neighbors) are the sound
  driver.
  FINDING 3 (limiting factor): pages hold 2-3 short acts; a single psm-6 pass
  reliably reads the FIRST header and drops lower ones. Mitigation: union psm
  6/4/11 in _scan_headers.headers_on_page(). Helps but does not fully close the
  gap -- some genuine headers still missed at 230 dpi.

  GO/NO-GO: GO overall (1935 arabic validation = 93.3% exact, the formal gate).
  The roman ENGINE is sound; the residual risk for biennials is page-LOCATION
  (multi-act pages + imperfect source_page), not number-READING. Application is
  run in CONFIRM-ONLY mode: a missing chapter is written ONLY when its exact
  oracle number is read as a clean header in its candidate page range -- so a
  miss costs coverage, never a wrong claim.

